<?xml version="1.0"?>
<section xml:id="game-of-life_conways-gol">
  <title>Conway's GoL</title>
  <p>One of the first cellular automatons to be studied, and probably the most popular of all time, is a 2-D CA called <q>The Game of Life</q>, or GoL for short. It was developed by John H. Conway and popularized in 1970 in Martin Gardner's column in <em>Scientific American</em>.</p>
  <p>The cells in GoL are arranged in a 2-D <idx>grid</idx><term>grid</term>, that is, an array of rows and columns. Usually the grid is considered to be infinite, but in practice it is often <q>wrapped</q>; that is, the right edge is connected to the left, and the top edge to the bottom.</p>
  <p>Each cell in the grid has two states &#x2014; live and dead &#x2014; and 8 neighbors &#x2014; north, south, east, west, and the four diagonals. This set of neighbors is sometimes called a <q>Moore neighborhood</q>.</p>
  <p>Like the 1-D CAs in the previous chapters, GoL evolves over time according to rules, which are like simple laws of physics.</p>
  <p>In GoL, the next state of each cell depends on its current state and its number of live neighbors. If a cell is alive, it stays alive if it has 2 or 3 neighbors, and dies otherwise. If a cell is dead, it stays dead unless it has exactly 3 neighbors.</p>
  <p>This behavior is loosely analogous to real cell growth: cells that are isolated or overcrowded die; at moderate densities they flourish.</p>
  <p>GoL is popular because:</p>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>There are simple initial conditions that yield surprisingly complex behavior.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>There are many interesting stable patterns: some oscillate (with various periods) and some move like the spaceships in Wolfram's Rule 110 CA.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>And like Rule 110, GoL is Turing complete.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Another factor that generated interest was Conway's conjecture &#x2014; that there is no initial condition that yields unbounded growth in the number of live cells &#x2014; and the $50 bounty he offered to anyone who could prove or disprove it.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Finally, the increasing availability of computers made it possible to automate the computation and display the results graphically.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>In the Game of life, cells are arranged in a 2-D grid and each cell in the grid has two states &#x2014; live and dead. Keeping that in mind, fill in the blanks, please give the numeral not the spelling and list the values in increasing order. </p>


       <p>In GoL (Game of life), If a cell is alive, it stays alive if it has <BlankNode/><var/> or <BlankNode/><var/> neighbors, and dies otherwise. If a cell is dead, it stays dead unless it has exactly <BlankNode/><var/> neighbors.</p>

    </statement>
    <setup>


      <var>
  <condition number="2">
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
      <condition string="^\s*two\s*$" case="insensitive">
        <feedback>
          <p>Please give the numeral, not the spelling.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
      
      <condition string=".*">
        <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
    </var>

      <var>
      <condition number="3">
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
      <condition string="^\s*three\s*$" case="insensitive">
        <feedback>
          <p>Please give the numeral, not the spelling.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
      <condition string=".*">
        <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
    </var>

    <var>
     <condition number="3">
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct, a dead cell needs exactly 3 neighbors to become alive.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
      <condition string="^\s*three\s*$" case="insensitive">
        <feedback>
          <p>Please give the numeral, not the spelling.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
      <condition string=".*">
        <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect, try again.</p>
        </feedback>
      </condition>
    </var>

    </setup>

  </exercise>
  <exercise>
     <statement>
      <p>Which of these were the reasons the author says GoL is popular? .</p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Simple initial conditions can produce surprisingly complex behavior.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct! The author lists this as one of the reasons GoL is popular.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>There are many interesting stable patterns, including oscillators and spaceships.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct! Stable and moving patterns are one of the reasons the author gives.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>GoL is Turing complete.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct! Being Turing complete is one of the reasons the author lists.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>Conway's conjecture and the $50 bounty he offered generated interest in the game.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct! The author mentions this as one of the reasons GoL became popular.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>GoL was the first cellular automaton ever developed by a computer scientist.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect. The text says GoL was one of the first cellular automatons to be studied, not the first ever developed.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>GoL was originally designed to model real biological cells and predict cell growth accurately.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect. The text says the behavior is only "loosely analogous" to real cell growth, and popularity was not attributed to biological accuracy.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
</section>
